0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","5.10",{"topic":{"id":"5.10","title":"AI-Assisted Digital Forensic Investigation","minutes":14,"summary":"Let AI assist triage and pattern discovery while preserving originals, provenance, and an examiner-controlled chain of reasoning.","sections":[{"title":"Useful assistance, clear boundaries","body":["AI can help rank files, cluster similar images or messages, identify likely relevant entities, transcribe audio, and summarize large log collections. These are assistance tasks, not automatic findings of guilt. Define the input, output, confidence, and review step. Preserve the original evidence and record the model version and settings used.","A transcript can contain recognition errors, especially with accents, overlapping speakers, or poor audio. A translated summary can change nuance. An entity extractor can confuse people and organizations. Important statements must be checked against the original recording or text, with uncertainty retained where the source is unclear."]},{"title":"Provenance and reproducibility","body":["Treat model output as a derived artifact linked to its input, model version, prompt or configuration, and execution time. Hash significant inputs and outputs where appropriate. A nondeterministic model may not produce identical wording on every run; reproducibility then includes the recorded output and process, not a promise of identical future generation.","Keep the model’s suggestions separate from verified observations. A generated timeline should not silently fill gaps. If an output cites an exhibit, verify that the exhibit exists and says what the summary claims. Do not allow a model to rewrite the acquisition log, custody record, or certificate as if it had witnessed the process."]},{"title":"Bias, review, and testimony","body":["Training data and feature choices can bias triage toward certain languages, users, or file types. Evaluate error patterns and provide a route to challenge results. Human review must be meaningful: the reviewer needs access to the source, the ability to disagree, and enough time to inspect material decisions.","An expert using AI should be able to explain the system’s role, validation, limitations, and the independent evidence supporting the conclusion. “The AI said so” is not a forensic method. Where the model cannot be adequately explained or tested, reduce the weight placed on it and seek corroborating methods."]}],"practice":{"title":"Audit a generated summary","scenario":"A model produces a confident timeline from a partial mailbox export.","steps":["Compare every timestamp and actor with the source.","Mark missing events and unsupported attribution.","Record the model and prompt as a derived-processing step.","Rewrite the summary with source references and explicit uncertainty."],"debrief":"Fluent language can conceal weak evidence. The examiner’s job is to test the summary, not polish an unsupported conclusion."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","dpdp"],"sources":["bsa","owasp-ml","privacy"],"labId":"ai","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain useful assistance, clear boundaries and apply it to an investigation.","Explain provenance and reproducibility and apply it to an investigation.","Explain bias, review, and testimony and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.10","prompt":"How should an AI-generated evidence summary be treated?","options":["As an independent original source.","As a derived aid requiring source-linked verification.","As a replacement for the custody record.","As proof of every statement it contains."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The summary depends on the input and model process; it does not become independent proof by being generated.","unit":5}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
